WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.720
Let's imagine for a moment we're walking together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.480
down the streets of a British town in&nbsp;
the early part of the 19th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.680
It's almost dawn, and the gas street lamps form&nbsp;
yellow globes of light in the early morning mist.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.360
Ahead, we see a figure begin to emerge from&nbsp;
the haze. It appears to be human but from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.160
body extends a long thin appendage&nbsp;
that reaches high into the gloom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.360
Is it an alien? And is that an antenna in&nbsp;
its pocket or is it just pleased to see us?

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:39.200
No, it's a man, and as he moves closer we see he's&nbsp;
carrying a long bamboo cane that he uses to tap on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:46.720
the upper floor window of each house he passes.&nbsp;
He's a knocker-up, also known as a knocker-upper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.160
someone hired to wake people in the morning&nbsp;
to ensure they get to work on time. Not to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.840
confused with the other type of knocker-upper who&nbsp;
does his job once and gets paid nine months later.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.640
Starting in the industrial era, in the times&nbsp;
before reliable and cost-effective alarm clocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.760
knocker-ups were the people who&nbsp;
got you out of bed in the morning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.480
Charles Dickens made reference to knocking up in&nbsp;
his novel Great Expectations, which makes sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.120
because life in Victorian England was&nbsp;
pretty shit for most people. It's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.560
surprise they needed a little extra&nbsp;
motivation to get the day started.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.800
Times have changed, but most of us still&nbsp;
find it hard to get going in the morning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.360
If you really struggle to get out of bed&nbsp;
each day, you may even have a condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:40.800
called dysania, which, although not yet medically&nbsp;
recognised, is a near complete inability to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.440
out of bed in the morning that’s thought to be&nbsp;
linked to other conditions like depression and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.360
chronic fatigue syndrome. And if you have an&nbsp;
overwhelming desire to stay in bed all day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.000
you might have a closely-related&nbsp;
condition called clinomania.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.040
Or you might just be horribly hungover.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.720
Regardless, waking up overly tired is&nbsp;
usually a sign that something’s wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.520
or you're not getting enough proper sleep,&nbsp;
which could simply be due to your lifestyle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.760
or perhaps one of approximately&nbsp;
80 different sleep disorders.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.760
For example, if someone wakes you while you’re&nbsp;
in a deep sleep, you'll probably feel a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.920
lost and disorientated. If this response is&nbsp;
particularly intense or occurs regularly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.200
you may be experiencing confusional arousals,&nbsp;
not to be mixed up with inappropriate arousal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.840
which is a terrible affliction experienced&nbsp;
by teenage boys at least three times a day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.480
Confusional arousal is a condition in which&nbsp;
you act in a very strange or confused way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.560
when you wake up. Episodes may&nbsp;
involve inappropriate behaviour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.960
shouting, or an apparent inability to "wake&nbsp;
up". Sounds like me on any normal day.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.600
Other sleep disorders are even more&nbsp;
bizarre. Have you ever suffered from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.440
Exploding Head Syndrome? Although it sounds&nbsp;
like a scene from a Quentin Tarantino film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.680
it actually isn't quite as&nbsp;
dramatic as you might think.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.360
Sufferers of this condition hear a sudden&nbsp;
and incredibly loud noise - like a gunshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:32.080
or a door slamming - just as they're drifting off&nbsp;
to sleep, or as they wake up from a deep sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.520
even though the room is completely silent

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.560
Every night you move through several sleep&nbsp;
cycles, with each cycle having five stages:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:52.480
four stages of non-rapid eye movement or non-REM&nbsp;
sleep, and one stage of REM sleep. It is during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:58.640
REM sleep that you experience dreams, and to stop&nbsp;
you acting out those fantasies in the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.040
your brain temporarily paralyses&nbsp;
your body - a process called atonia.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.560
Sleep paralysis happens when you gain&nbsp;
consciousness before your body has moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.480
fully out of the rapid eye&nbsp;
movement or REM stage of sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.600
and if you’ve ever experienced it you’ll know&nbsp;
it can be absolutely terrifying. In this state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:32.960
you’re semi-awake or even fully-awake,&nbsp;
but completely unable to move a muscle.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.000
And as if total immobility weren’t enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.920
there are even stranger symptoms of&nbsp;
this surprisingly common condition.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.840
As many as 40% of people experience sleep&nbsp;
paralysis at some point in their lives, and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:52.800
recall sensing a presence nearby or even on top&nbsp;
of them during the period of paralysis, causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:59.360
a feeling of intense pressure on their chest and&nbsp;
an inability to breathe. That presence has taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.280
many forms, usually shaped by the cultural beliefs&nbsp;
of the time. Characters have ranged from witches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:14.320
to trolls to ghosts, with aliens being the most&nbsp;
popular nighttime chest-sitters in the modern era.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:22.240
The condition is believed to be the origin of the&nbsp;
incubus and succubus myths, which warn of demons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.520
that attack during the night, usually sexually.&nbsp;
Sleep paralysis is also thought to have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:33.360
the inspiration for the famous 18th-century&nbsp;
painting by Henry Fuseli - no relation to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.920
pasta - called ''The Nightmare,'' which shows a&nbsp;
goblin sitting on the stomach of a sleeping woman.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:47.760
Confusional arousals, exploding head syndrome and&nbsp;
sleep paralysis are all examples of parasomnias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.480
a broad group of sleep disorders that&nbsp;
involve weird things happening while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.760
you’re falling asleep, sleeping or waking up.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.200
They can include strange dreams,&nbsp;
emotions, movements, or behaviours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.560
and most of them happen during non-REM sleep.&nbsp;
They can sometimes be traumatic for the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:12.560
involved or the people they live with, but for the&nbsp;
most part, these conditions are fairly harmless.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.880
Take sleep-talking, for instance. Sure, you&nbsp;
may say a few embarrassing things out loud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.240
while asleep on the train, but seriously, if&nbsp;
you like rubbing peanut butter on your nipples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.760
you shouldn’t be embarrased to tell&nbsp;
the world about it while unconscious.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.800
Or how about sleep sex, also known as sexsomnia?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.080
Nothing wrong with wanting to get&nbsp;
your jollies off mid-slumber is there?

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:42.480
Then there’s sleepwalking. Everyone knows somebody&nbsp;
with a funny sleepwalking story. But surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.360
apart from the risk of walking into things,&nbsp;
peeing in your cupboard or falling down stairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:49.920
it's all just a bit of fun?

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:59.520
Well, yes, most manifestations of parasomnias&nbsp;
have little impact in the wider world. But in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:07.280
alarming cases, they have ended in violent,&nbsp;
even fatal, criminal acts being committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.160
under the supposed blanket of sleep.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:16.480
The defendants in these cases have sworn they&nbsp;
were asleep when committing their crimes and knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:22.080
nothing of their actions, an argument that has&nbsp;
come to be known as the 'sleepwalking defence.'

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:28.720
Sleepwalking, technically known as somnambulism,&nbsp;
usually occurs during slow-wave sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:36.400
the deepest stages of non-REM sleep. Exactly&nbsp;
what causes it isn’t known, but essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.160
the brain seems to get stuck&nbsp;
between sleep and wakefulness.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:46.320
Recently, some researchers have provided an&nbsp;
alternative theory that, instead of being awake or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:55.120
asleep, sleepwalkers are both at the same time in&nbsp;
what might be a new type of state disassociation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.920
with consciousness and unconsciousness&nbsp;
switching rapidly and continuously.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.320
Either way, sleepwalkers can do a lot more&nbsp;
than walk. They may sit up and look around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.200
pick up objects around the home, take a&nbsp;
stroll out of the house or even drive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:18.080
There’s a common myth that you shouldn't wake&nbsp;
a sleepwalker, when in fact leaving someone to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:26.000
sleepwalk can lead to disastrous, even homicidal,&nbsp;
consequences, as we’re about to find out.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.720
The first legal record of sleepwalking&nbsp;
being used as a successful defence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.480
was in the United States in 1846. In&nbsp;
what became known as The Boston Tragedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.800
Albert Tirrell slit the throat of the woman&nbsp;
he’d left his wife for, a prostitute named Maria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:49.840
Bickford, almost decapitating her in the process.&nbsp;
Then, just to prove he meant business, he set fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:56.080
to the brothel and fled to New Orleans, where he&nbsp;
was arrested. In court, his lawyer argued that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:02.400
Tirrell was a longtime and chronic sleepwalker&nbsp;
and perhaps committed the crime while asleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:08.800
And the jury obviously thought it was a&nbsp;
plausible story, because Tirrell was acquitted.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:17.360
In 1887,esteemed Paris detective Robert Ledru was&nbsp;
sent to Normandy to help solve an ongoing case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:23.600
He arrived at night and went to bed early, but&nbsp;
woke the next morning to find his shoes and socks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.920
were soaked through. He also discovered&nbsp;
the case he’d been assigned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:34.880
was no longer a priority because, during the&nbsp;
night, there had been a murdah – a murdah?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:42.960
Aye, a murdah. A prominent businessman had&nbsp;
been shot on the beach, with no clues as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:49.280
why. Ledru investigated, taking plaster casts&nbsp;
of the footprints that led to the corpse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:57.360
He was naturally more than a little surprised to&nbsp;
discover the footprints exactly matched his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:05.360
and combined with his inexplicably wet socks and&nbsp;
shoes, he came to the startling conclusion he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.560
was the murderer, despite having no&nbsp;
recollection whatsoever of the crime.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:15.440
His findings were confirmed by ballistics&nbsp;
testing, and he was imprisoned for his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:22.560
good. During his incarceration, prison guards&nbsp;
gave him a gun loaded with blanks, and one night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:28.080
Ledru suddenly got up and started firing at&nbsp;
the man guarding his cell. It was enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:35.600
convince the authorities that he was a homicidal&nbsp;
sleepwalker, and not a cold blooded killer. Ledru&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:42.240
was moved to a secluded farm outside Paris&nbsp;
and kept there until his death 50 years later.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:48.240
Perhaps the most well-known of these&nbsp;
cases occurred in 1987 when Kenneth Parks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.480
a young Canadian man, was acquitted of murder&nbsp;
on the grounds of sleepwalking. One night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:59.440
Parks climbed into his car and drove&nbsp;
14 miles to his parents-in-law’s house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:06.720
where he proceeded to strangle, beat and stab his&nbsp;
mother and father-in-law, with whom he apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:13.360
had close relationships. His mother-in-law was&nbsp;
killed, and his father-in-law seriously injured.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:19.360
Afterwards, still dripping with blood, Parks drove&nbsp;
to a police station and confessed to the crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:23.120
but witnesses said he appeared&nbsp;
confused about what had happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:29.440
He had also severed tendons in both hands during&nbsp;
the attack and should have been feeling intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:36.000
pain, but he seemed altogether unaware of&nbsp;
his injuries. At the trial, the jury was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.320
convinced Parks had indeed been unconscious&nbsp;
at the time, and he was found not guilty.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:48.880
More recently, in 2008, British man Brian&nbsp;
Thomas was accused of the murder of his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:54.560
who he admitted he’d strangled in the back of&nbsp;
their campervan. Thomas couldn't remember what had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:00.880
happened but thought he had been dreaming that his&nbsp;
wife was an intruder. The prosecution eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:06.880
withdrew the charges, and the judge said that&nbsp;
Thomas wasn't responsible for his actions.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:12.800
But before you start planning to bump off&nbsp;
that annoying guy at work who always steals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:18.160
your parking space before giving an exaggerated&nbsp;
yawn and claiming you were asleep the whole time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:23.840
it’s worth bearing in mind not everyone&nbsp;
gets away with the sleepwalking defence.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:31.680
In 1997 in Phoenix, Arizona, Scott Falater&nbsp;
stabbed his wife 44 times with a hunting knife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:36.240
His neighbour also testified to seeing&nbsp;
him drag his wife's body to the pool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:41.920
before holding her head underwater. Falater&nbsp;
claimed to have been asleep at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:48.080
saying he had a history of sleepwalking and had&nbsp;
been sleep-deprived in the run up to the incident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:53.360
The prosecution argued Falater had tried to&nbsp;
hide evidence of the murder in a way that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:59.120
too complex to have been achieved while asleep.&nbsp;
And in between the stabbing and the drowning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:04.480
a neighbour also saw Falater motion for&nbsp;
his dog to lie down, which was considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:11.040
a possible sign of consciousness. A jury found&nbsp;
him guilty of first-degree murder in 1999.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:18.480
In Spain in 2001, Antonio Nieto brutally&nbsp;
killed his wife and mother-in-law. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.120
daughter and son tried to stop him, and both&nbsp;
received severe injuries for their efforts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:29.680
Nieto told the court he had been asleep during&nbsp;
the attack and had been dreaming he was defending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:36.080
himself against aggressive ostriches. Full&nbsp;
marks for inventiveness there Antonio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:41.120
But his children said he had recognised them&nbsp;
during the attack and had even told them not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:46.080
to turn the light on because their mother,&nbsp;
who had already been brutally assaulted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:52.160
was supposedly sleeping. Nieto was sentenced&nbsp;
to 10 years in a psychiatric facility.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:59.200
These are only a few of more than 100 examples&nbsp;
of apparent sleep-related violence that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:04.640
been reported in the last century or so,&nbsp;
which include instances of homicide, assault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:10.800
and sexual assault. For a long time, doctors and&nbsp;
lawyers lumped all types of bizarre sleep violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:16.560
under the heading of sleepwalking, but there&nbsp;
are distinct differences between somnambulism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:21.920
and another type of parasomnia:&nbsp;
REM behaviour disorder, or RBD.

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.240
REM behaviour disorder happens during&nbsp;
REM sleep while someone is dreaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:30.000
but, instead of the muscles&nbsp;
being paralysed as usual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.320
the dreamer's body is still active and&nbsp;
they're able to act out their dreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:38.800
When they’re woken up, they can usually&nbsp;
remember fragments of the dream they were in.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:46.000
This is different from sleepwalkers who, operating&nbsp;
from non-REM sleep, usually have no recollection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:52.080
of what happened once they’re woken and are&nbsp;
in general more confused and disorientated.

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:58.480
Somnambulism and RBD are quite different,&nbsp;
but I for one wouldn’t be overly interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:02.480
in the semantics if someone in a&nbsp;
nightgown was chasing me with an axe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:05.600
So why do these types of distinctions matter?

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.520
In the end, they help to determine&nbsp;
the tricky business of truth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:13.600
Up until fairly recently, it was&nbsp;
impossible to test whether someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:18.720
was telling the truth when they claimed to&nbsp;
have been asleep during a violent episode.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:24.080
Traditionally, courts have relied on historical&nbsp;
and circumstantial evidence to make the call.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:26.640
Sleepwalking, for example, is partly genetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:31.440
and sufferers of RBD or somnambulism&nbsp;
often have a history of the condition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:37.840
Sleep deprivation, stress, alcohol, and drugs can&nbsp;
also make someone more susceptible to an episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:43.600
Sleepwalking is also often triggered by a sound,&nbsp;
heavy snoring being the most common. All of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:48.960
points have in the past been used as indicators&nbsp;
as to whether a defendant is telling the truth.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.280
But technological advancements have&nbsp;
made it possible for scientists to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:59.360
accurate assessments about whether someone&nbsp;
is a genuine sleepwalker or just a sleepliar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.560
Three lab tests are used to assess when&nbsp;
someone is in different stages of sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:12.400
The first, an electroencephalogram (EEG), records&nbsp;
brain activity in the form of waves. The second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:19.760
an electrooculogram (EOG), detects eye&nbsp;
movements, and finally, an electromyogram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:26.080
(EMG) measures muscle activity via a sensor&nbsp;
placed on the chin, or an arm or leg.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:30.560
Of course, the number of people who&nbsp;
have committed a crime while asleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:37.600
and wearing these different sensors is pretty low&nbsp;
- OK zero - so we don't have definitive proof of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:43.600
whether a potential murderer was asleep or not&nbsp;
when they acted violently. But, being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:51.120
retrospectively assess people's sleep patterns&nbsp;
can help to establish probability. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:55.920
you know, if someone goes to sleep every night&nbsp;
repeatedly slapping his penis as he drifts off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.920 --> 00:18:00.560
he’s probably more partial to a spot of sleep&nbsp;
murder than someone who sleeps like a log.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:06.080
Neuroimaging studies have also started to advance&nbsp;
our understanding of how sleepwalking works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:11.440
Claudio Bassetti of the University of Zurich&nbsp;
has examined the brains of sleepwalkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:15.920
using single-photon emission&nbsp;
computerised tomography, or SPECT.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:19.360
He has discovered that, in sleepwalking states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:24.240
your brain's cerebrum and cerebral cortex -&nbsp;
the clever parts of your brain that decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.760
the difference between right and wrong -&nbsp;
shut down. Your brain becomes directed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:36.160
more primitive and automatic functions, the&nbsp;
kind connected to hunger, sex, and survival.

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:41.360
From a legal point of view, it confirms&nbsp;
that sleepwalkers and sufferers of RBD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:48.560
are not conscious when they’re asleep and,&nbsp;
so cannot be considered to have mens rea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:54.240
which is the intention to commit a crime and is&nbsp;
typically required for someone to be found guilty.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:58.960
We’re still some way off from having a&nbsp;
foolproof way of deciding whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:03.440
someone was genuinely asleep when they&nbsp;
committed some terrible act of violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:10.160
but we do now know that this is a real thing.&nbsp;
So, until we understand more, it might be an idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:16.160
to hide the kitchen knives before you switch the&nbsp;
lights out each night. And, of course, sleep well.

